# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A raw delimiter carries a scope themes colour"

# Across the shipped grammars a verbatim delimiter takes its colour either from the region it
# opens, or from a scope of its own — shell gives a heredoc marker keyword.operator.heredoc,
# Perl gives POD constant.language. Which one serves a reader depends on whether themes reach
# the region.
#
# A BLOCK region carries meta.* alongside markup.fenced_code.block, and 38 of 65 themes leave a
# delimiter nesting there uncoloured, so a fence and a typed block keep a scope of their own. It
# reads keyword.control rather than keyword.operator: six of the fifteen shipped themes set
# keyword.operator to the default foreground, and every one of them colours keyword.control.
#
# An INLINE tick opens a markup.inline.raw region, which themes do reach, so the tick nests
# there and paints with the code it wraps — the shape markdown, asciidoc and mdx all write.
# Carrying its own keyword scope instead, it matched its own code in none of the 65 themes.

```js
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.codeblock.tiddlywiki5 keyword.control.raw.fence.tiddlywiki5 punctuation.definition.raw.markup.begin.tiddlywiki5
var a = 1;
```

A paragraph carrying `inline code` and ``a double tick``.
#                    ^ text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5 markup.inline.raw.single.tiddlywiki5 punctuation.definition.raw.markup.single.begin.tiddlywiki5
#                                      ^^ text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5 markup.inline.raw.double.tiddlywiki5 punctuation.definition.raw.markup.double.begin.tiddlywiki5

$$$text/plain
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.typedblock.tiddlywiki5 keyword.control.raw.typedblock.tiddlywiki5 punctuation.definition.typedblock.begin.tiddlywiki5
a typed block
$$$
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.typedblock.tiddlywiki5 keyword.control.raw.typedblock.tiddlywiki5 punctuation.definition.typedblock.end.tiddlywiki5
